"Internal Error ()\nciteturn5view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.joppebos.com/montgomery.html\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://www.joppebos.com/montgomery.html is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn5view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck/releases\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck/releases: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn5view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck/main/LICENSE-MIT\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://raw.githubusercontent.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck/main/LICENSE-MIT: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn5view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/snarang181/zk-duel/master/LICENSE\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://raw.githubusercontent.com/snarang181/zk-duel/master/LICENSE: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.joppebos.com/montgomery/book_ch1.pdf)\nciteturn5view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 18; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn4view0\",\"pattern\":\"1.4\"}); Total lines: 433\nL0@P0: Introduction to\nL1@P0: Topics in computational number theory\nL2@P0: inspired by Peter L. Montgomery\nL3@P0: Joppe W. Bos, Arjen K. Lenstra, Herman te Riele, and Daniel Shumow\nL4@P0: NXP Semiconductors, Leuven, Belgium\nL5@P0: EPFL, Lausanne, Switzerland\nL6@P0: CWI, Amsterdam, Netherlands\nL7@P0: Microsoft Research, Redmond, USA\nL8@P0: Abstract\nL9@P0: This article appeared as the introductory chapter of the book Topics in Compu\u0002tational Number Theory inspired by Peter L. Montgomery, edited by Joppe W.\nL10@P0: Bos and Arjen K. Lenstra and published by Cambridge University Press. See\nL11@P0: www.cambridge.org/9781107109353. It contains multiple cross-references\nL12@P0-1: to the other chapters of the same book.Contents\nL13@P1: 1 Introduction page 1\nL14@P1: 1.1 Outline 1\nL15@P1: 1.2 Biographical sketch 1\nL16@P1: 1.3 Overview 5\nL17@P1: 1.4 Simultaneous inversion 8\nL18@P1: Bibliography 11\nL19@P1-3: Subject index 151\nL20@P3: Introduction\nL21@P3: Joppe W. Bos, Arjen K. Lenstra, Herman te Riele and Daniel Shumow\nL22@P3: 1.1 Outline\nL23@P3: This introductory chapter collects some personal background on Peter L. Mont\u0002gomery, both provided by himself and based on stories from his colleagues and\nL24@P3: collaborators. This is followed by a brief introduction to the other chapters, and\nL25@P3: by a description of Peter’s simultaneous inversion method.\nL26@P3: 1.2 Biographical sketch\nL27@P3: Peter was born in San Francisco, California, on September 25, 1947. He de\u0002scribed his activities since his 1965 graduation from San Rafael High School\nL28@P3: (SRHS) in a letter for a 1995 high school reunion; this letter is copied in its\nL29@P3: entirety in Figure 1.1, and further commented on throughout this chapter.\nL30@P3: At Berkeley, Peter’s undergraduate advisor was Derrick H. Lehmer, an ex\u0002cellent match given Peter’s research interests since high school. Ron Gra\u0002ham, one of Peter’s coauthors on his first three papers (all on Ramsey theory\nL31@P3: and written shortly after his time in Berkeley), recalls [25] that Peter had the\nL32@P3: “breakthrough idea” for the first of these papers, with coauthor Bruce Roth\u0002schild remembering Peter “as an extremely clever guy who came up with sur\u0002prising ideas” [51]. The papers in question ([22], [23] and [24]) were further\nL33@P3: coauthored by Paul Erdös, Joel Spencer, and Ernst Straus. After this auspi\u0002cious start, Peter’s next paper describes an algorithm that he developed at SDC\nL34@P3: to evaluate arbitrary Fortran boolean expressions in such a way that they re\u0002sult in a small number of instructions when compiled on a Control Data 7600\nL35@P3: computer [35]. The paper is illustrative of the type of tinkering in which Peter\nL36@P3: excels.\nL37@P3-4: About twenty years after his time in Berkeley, and by the time that Pe-2\nL38@P4: Figure 1.1 Peter’s letter for his 1995 high-school reunion\nL39@P4: ter was already “internationally famous” (cf. Figure 1.1), David G. Cantor\nL40@P4: supervised Peter’s PhD dissertation An FFT extension of the elliptic curve\nL41@P4: method of factorization [38]. Milos Ercegovac, who served on the PhD com\u0002mittee, related [21] that Cantor referred to Peter as “a genius UCLA never\nL42@P4: saw before”, how Peter was brilliant in solving engineering problems related\nL43@P4-5: to computer arithmetic, and that, as a result of his incisive reviews, authorsIntroduction 3\nL44@P5: (a) At age 15 (b) In 2009\nL45@P5: Figure 1.2 Peter L. Montgomery (from Peter’s collection and Wikipedia)\nL102@P7: contributions and his work as a programmer were instrumental to these massive\nL103@P7: advances that spanned his career – a career of more than forty years, impressive\nL104@P7: by the standards of the software industry.\nL105@P7: 1.3 Overview\nL106@P7: The subsequent chapters of this book are on active research areas that were ini\u0002tiated by Peter or to which he has made substantial contributions. This section\nL107@P7: contains an overview of the subjects that are treated.\nL108@P7: As mentioned above, Peter’s 1985 method for modular multiplication with\u0002out trial division [36] was invented almost serendipitously when Peter tried to\nL109@P7: put otherwise idle registers to good use. Because it allows simple implement\u0002ation both in software and in hardware, Montgomery multiplication (as the\nL110@P7: method is now commonly referred to) has found broad application. Its soft\u0002ware and hardware aspects are covered in chapters 2 and 3, respectively.\nL111@P7: Peter’s contributions as described and expanded upon in chapters 4 through 8\nL112@P7: were all inspired by integer factorization, his main research interest since high\nL113@P7: school. In [37, Section 10.3.1] Peter describes how his original implementation\nL114@P7-8: of the elliptic curve method of integer factorization [33] uses the Weierstrass6\nL115@P8: equation with affine coordinates, and how the modular inversion required for\nL116@P8: the point addition can be made faster if several curves are run at once (cf. Sec\u0002tion 1.4 on page 8). He then writes “The author later discovered an alternative\nL117@P8: parametrization that requires no inversions” and continues with the description\nL118@P8: of what is now known as Montgomery curves and the Montgomery ladder.\nL119@P8: The former is an elliptic curve parametrization that allows fast computation of\nL120@P8: the sum of two points if their difference is known and that does not require\nL121@P8: modular inversion. The latter refers to the addition chain that can then be used\nL122@P8: to compute any scalar multiple of a point. Initially these results were relevant\nL123@P8: solely to those implementing the elliptic curve integer factorization method,\nL124@P8: but the later growing importance of elliptic curve cryptography increased the\nL125@P8: interest in Peter’s and yet other curve parametrizations and addition chains.\nL126@P8: Chapter 4 describes the developments in this field that have taken place since\nL127@P8: the publication of [37].\nL128@P8: In parallel with his work on special purpose integer factoring methods in\nL129@P8: [37], Peter also heavily influenced the two most important methods of the\nL130@P8: more generic integer factorization algorithms that are described in Chapter 5:\nL131@P8: the quadratic sieve and the number field sieve, two methods that were ini\u0002tially plagued by severe performance problems [48, 31]. Although Peter was\nL132@P8: not the first to propose a practical version of the quadratic sieve, his multi\u0002ple polynomial quadratic sieve (as published by Robert Silverman in [54] and\nL133@P8: described in Chapter 5), represented the state of the art in integer factoriza\u0002tion from the mid 1980s until the early 1990s. It was later enhanced by self\u0002initialization [50], but by that time the number field sieve had taken over as the\nL134@P8: record-breaking factoring method, a position it holds to the present day. Peter\nL135@P8: played a prominent role in that development, contributing important ideas to\nL136@P8: all steps of the number field sieve: polynomial selection, (early) sieving, build\u0002ing a matrix, processing the matrix, and the final square root calculation. A\nL137@P8: brief summary follows, with Chapter 5 describing in more detail how the steps\nL138@P8: fit together.\nL139@P8: In the first step of the number field sieve two or more polynomials must be\nL140@P8: found that have a root in common modulo the number to be factored. Though\nL141@P8: theoretically such polynomials are easily found, small modifications may trig\u0002ger orders of magnitude reductions of the resulting factoring effort. Finding\nL142@P8: good polynomials has become a subject in its own right since Peter’s early\nL143@P8: contributions (many of which were first published in Brian Murphy’s 1999\nL144@P8: PhD dissertation [45]). Chapter 6 is entirely devoted to the current state of the\nL145@P8: art of polynomial selection for the number field sieve.\nL146@P8: The number field sieve (and many other sieving-based integer factorization\nL147@P8-9: methods) relies on the ability to quickly recognize which values in an arith-Introduction 7\nL148@P9: metic progression have many small factors. Chapter 5 briefly mentions some\nL149@P9: of Peter’s many tricks which are now part of the standard repertoire to speed up\nL150@P9: this so-called sieving step. His improvements to the earliest line sieving soft\u0002ware for the number field sieve by the second author were used for numerous\nL151@P9: integer factorizations in the 1990s [10, 32]. For most composites, however, line\nL152@P9: sieving has since then been surpassed by lattice sieving [47].\nL153@P9: The sieving step results in a large, very sparse matrix. In the matrix step de\u0002pendencies among the columns of this matrix must be found. Given its sparsity,\nL154@P9: it is advantageous to first transform the matrix into an equivalent matrix that is\nL155@P9: smaller but less sparse. Originally, when regular Gaussian elimination was still\nL156@P9: used for the matrix step, a set of ad hoc methods referred to as structured Gaus\u0002sian elimination [29, 49] was used to find a transformed matrix with a smallest\nL157@P9: possible number of rows, but without paying attention to its density. The more\nL158@P9: sophisticated tools that became available for the matrix step in the mid 1990s\nL159@P9: required an adaption of the matrix transformation method that would minimize\nL160@P9: the product of the number of rows of the matrix and its number of non-zero\nL161@P9: entries. These days this is referred to as filtering. The details of the various fil\u0002tering steps proposed by Peter (and based on the earlier work in [29, 49]) can\nL162@P9: be found in [12, 13] and are sketched in Chapter 5.\nL163@P9: The above “more sophisticated tools” are the block Wiedemann and block\nL164@P9: Lanczos methods, based on the classical Wiedemann and Lanczos methods.\nL165@P9: They were almost simultaneously and independently developed by Don Cop\u0002persmith and Peter, respectively, and have comparable performance [17, 40].\nL166@P9: Though both methods allow efficient distributed implementation, for block\nL167@P9: Lanczos all processors must be able to communicate quickly (i.e., a single\nL168@P9: cluster with a fast interconnection network), whereas block Wiedemann can be\nL169@P9: made to run efficiently on a modest number of disjoint clusters. Almost since\nL170@P9: its inception in the mid 1990s, block Lanczos became the method of choice\nL171@P9: for all academic factoring projects. Around 2005 it became more convenient\nL172@P9: for these applications to use a number of separate clusters for the matrix step,\nL173@P9: which implied a switch to block Wiedemann [27, 28]. Chapter 7 describes the\nL174@P9: block Lanczos method in detail.\nL175@P9: For the number field sieve each column dependency produced by the matrix\nL176@P9: step requires a square root calculation in the number fields involved before a\nL177@P9: factorization of the targeted composite may be derived. In [39] Peter presented\nL178@P9: an iterative approach that cleverly exploits the special form of the squared al\u0002gebraic numbers, thereby replacing Jean-Marc Couveignes’ much slower and\nL179@P9: more restrictive method [18]. Peter’s method is used, essentially unmodified,\nL180@P9: until the present day, and sketched in Chapter 5.\nL181@P9-10: Returning to special purpose integer factorization, in the last paragraph of8\nL182@P10: his p−1 paper [46] John Pollard mentions “we would theoretically use the fast\nL183@P10: Fourier transform on Step 2”, concluding his paper with “I cannot say at what\nL184@P10: point this becomes a practical proposition”. In [37, Section 4] Peter refers to\nL185@P10: Pollard’s remark, and further pursues the idea for Pollard’s p − 1 method, first\nL186@P10: with Silverman in [44] and later with Alexander Kruppa in [42]. For the elliptic\nL187@P10: curve factoring method it is the subject of Peter’s PhD dissertation [38]. Peter’s\nL188@P10: work on this subject is presented in Chapter 8.\nL189@P10: In Chapter 9 Peter’s contributions to cryptographic pairings are outlined.\nL190@P10: Given Peter’s simultaneous inversion method (described below in Section 1.4)\nL191@P10: and his fast modular inversion it became attractive, contrary to conventional\nL192@P10: wisdom, to replace projective coordinates by affine ones for applications in\u0002volving multiple or parallelized pairings, products of pairings, or pairings at\nL193@P10: high security levels [19, 20, 30]. Moreover, Peter coauthored a paper intro\u0002ducing algorithms to compute the squared pairing, which has the advantage,\nL194@P10: compared to Victor Miller’s algorithm, that its computation cannot fail [34].\nL195@P10: 1.4 Simultaneous inversion\nL196@P10: For completeness, this section describes Peter Montgomery’s simultaneous in\u0002version method, a popular method that did not lead to any follow-up work and\nL197@P10: that is therefore not treated in the later chapters.\nL198@P10: At a computational number theory conference held in August 1985 at Hum\u0002boldt State University in Arcata, CA, Peter gave a presentation about his ex\u0002perience using the elliptic curve integer factorization method ([33], informally\nL199@P10: announced in February 1985). He proposed to maximize the overall throughput\nL200@P10: of the method as opposed to minimizing the latency per curve, thus exploiting\nL201@P10: the method’s inherent parallelism. The idea is simple: at a modest overhead the\nL202@P10: cost of the modular inversion required for each elliptic curve group operation\nL203@P10: in the Weierstrass model can be shared among any number of simultaneously\nL204@P10: processed curves, assuming all curves target the same number to be factored. It\nL205@P10: has become known as simultaneous inversion, and was later briefly mentioned\nL206@P10: in [37, Section 10.3.1]:\nL207@P10: When working over several curves, the program used a scheme similar to (1.2) to do\nL208@P10: all the inversions at once, since (1/x) = y(1/xy) and (1/y) = x(1/xy). This reduces the\nL209@P10: asymptotic cost of an inversion to that of 3 multiplications.\nL210@P10: The “scheme similar to (1.2)” suggests that Peter took his inspiration from\nL211@P10-11: John Pollard’s observation that the cost of a number of greatest common divi\u0002sor calculations with the same to-be-factored modulus can be reduced to thatIntroduction 9\nL212@P11: same number of multiplications modulo the same modulus, plus a single gcd\nL213@P11: calculation. In the inversion context, there may have been a historical prece\u0002dent for Peter’s trick, with Richard Schroeppel recalling he may have seen a\nL214@P11: similar method to avoid floating divides in pre-1970s Fortran programs. But\nL215@P11: even if it is not entirely original, Peter’s insight cleverly adapts an old idea to a\nL216@P11: regime where numerics are exact [53]. These days, simultaneous inversion is,\nL217@P11: for instance, used in record computations of elliptic curve discrete logarithms\nL218@P11: (e.g., [6] and the record attempt [1]; it was not used in [55]).\nL219@P11: Let z1,z2, . . . ,zk ∈ Z/nZ for some modulus n be the elements that must be\nL220@P11: inverted; in the rare case that at least one of the inverses does not exist, simulta\u0002neous inversion fails, and an appropriate alternative approach is used. Working\nL221@P11: in Z/nZ and with w0 = 1, first for i = 1, 2, . . . , k in succession calculate and\nL222@P11: store wi = wi−1zi. Next, calculate w = w\nL223@P11: −1\nL224@P11: k\nL225@P11: . Finally, for i = k, k − 1, . . . , 1\nL226@P11: in succession first compute z\nL227@P11: −1\nL228@P11: i\nL229@P11: as wwi−1 and next replace w by wzi (so that\nL230@P11: w = w\nL231@P11: −1\nL232@P11: i−1\nL233@P11: ). The overall cost is 3(k − 1) multiplications, a single inversion, and\nL234@P11: storage for k temporary values, all in Z/nZ. Given that for relevant modulus\nL235@P11: sizes and software implementation of arithmetic in Z/nZ inversion is at least\nL236@P11: five times costlier than multiplication, the resulting speed up can be signif\u0002icant. One should be aware, though, that in hardware the difference can be\nL237@P11-13: made smaller [26].Bibliography\nL238@P13: [1] D. V. Bailey, L. Batina, D. J. Bernstein, P. Birkner, J. W. Bos, H.-C. Chen, C.-M.\nL239@P13: Cheng, G. van Damme, G. de Meulenaer, L. J. D. Perez, J. Fan, T. Güneysu,\nL240@P13: F. Gurkaynak, T. Kleinjung, T. Lange, N. Mentens, R. Niederhagen, C. Paar,\nL241@P13: F. Regazzoni, P. Schwabe, L. Uhsadel, A. V. Herrewege, and B.-Y. Yang. Break\u0002ing ECC2K-130. Cryptology ePrint Archive, Report 2009/541, 2009. http:\nL242@P13: //eprint.iacr.org/2009/541. (Cited on page 9.)\nL243@P13: [2] R. Barbulescu, J. W. Bos, C. Bouvier, T. Kleinjung, and P. L. Montgomery. Find\u0002ing ECM-friendly curves through a study of Galois properties. The Open Book\nL244@P13: Series – Proceedings of the Tenth Algorithmic Number Theory Symposium, pages\nL245@P13: 63–86, 2013. (Cited on page 4.)\nL246@P13: [3] C. Baum. The System Builders: The Story of SDC. System Development Corpo\u0002ration, 1981. (Cited on pages 4 and 5.)\nL247@P13: [4] E. R. Berlekamp. Algebraic coding theory. McGraw-Hill, 1968. (Cited on\nL248@P13: page 3.)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.joppebos.com/montgomery/book_ch1.pdf)\nciteturn5view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 18; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn4view0\",\"pattern\":\"2017\"}); Total lines: 433\nNo matching text found for \"2017\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - snarang181/zk-duel · GitHub (https://github.com/snarang181/zk-duel)\nciteturn5view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"pattern\":\"Goldilocks\"}); Total lines: 417\nL163: Name  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\nL164: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL165: 【63†benchmarks】  | 【63†benchmarks】  |    |\nL175: 【73†README.md】  | 【73†README.md】  |    |\nL176: 【74†pyproject.toml】  | 【74†pyproject.toml】  |    |\nL177: [Button: View all files]\nL178: ## Repository files navigation\nL179: \nL180:   *   * 【75†README】\nL181:   * 【75†MIT license】\nL182: \nL183: More items\nL184: \nL185: # zkDuel\nL186: \nL187: zkDuel is the artifact of the paper zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models (HASP '26). It contains matched Triton and CUDA SumCheck prover kernels over prime fields, the drivers that time and check them, and the data behind the paper's results.\nL188: \nL189: Repository: 【47†https://github.com/snarang181/zk-duel】\nL190: ## What zkDuel is\nL191: \nL192:   * Matched kernels. Each workload has a Triton implementation and a native CUDA implementation of the same SumCheck round: evaluate the round polynomial at its interpolation points, reduce the partial sums, fold the tables with the challenge. Both backends issue the same kernel launches and host synchronizations per run. The CUDA code builds to a standalone executable; the Python drivers run it as a subprocess, with no Python bindings.\nL193:   * Workloads. 25 constraints derived from zkPHIRE, in seven families, plus a diagnostic tier of 7 polynomials. Both sets are listed below.\nL194: \nL195:   * Fields. Four prime fields in Montgomery form with 32-bit limbs:\nL196: Width  | Prime  | Limbs\nL197: --- | --- | ---\nL198: 32  | 2^32 − 5  | 1\nL199: 64  | Goldilocks, 2^64 − 2^32 + 1  | 2\nL200: 128  | 2^127 − 1  | 4\nL201: 256  | BLS12-381 scalar field (255-bit prime)  | 8\nL202:   * Correctness gating. A configuration counts as a result only when both backends finish and their checksums agree. Both backends are also checked against a CPU big-integer reference computed from the canonical expressions in `src/zkduel/workloads.py`.\nL203:   * Measurements. Median wall-clock time per SumCheck run, GPU-kernel time from Nsight Systems, registers and occupancy, and Triton compile time.\nL204: \nL205: zkPHIRE constraints (`--zk-only` selects all 25; the aliases `zkphire_0` to `zkphire_24` are also accepted):\nL206: Family  | n  | Workloads\nL207: --- | --- | ---\nL208: Verifiable / Spartan  | 3  | `zk_verifiable_asics`, `zk_spartan_1`, `zk_spartan_2`\nL209: Halo2 curve checks  | 3  | `zk_witness_non_id`, `zk_witness_id_point_1`, `zk_witness_id_point_2`\nL210: Incomplete addition  | 2  | `zk_incomplete_add_1`, `zk_incomplete_add_2`\nL211: Complete addition  | 12  | `zk_complete_add_1` to `zk_complete_add_12`\nL212: HyperPlonk vanilla  | 2  | `zk_vanilla_zerocheck_hp`, `zk_vanilla_permcheck_hp`\nL213: HyperPlonk Jellyfish  | 2  | `zk_jellyfish_zerocheck_hp`, `zk_jellyfish_permcheck_hp`\nL214: Opening check  | 1  | `zk_opencheck`\nL215: Diagnostic tier:\nL216: \nL217: Workload  | Polynomial\nL218: --- | ---\nL219: `poly_a`  | `a`\nL220: `poly_ab`  | `a*b`\nL221: `poly_ab_plus_c`  | `a*b + c`\nL222: `poly_abc`  | `a*b*c`\nL223: `poly_aabbc`  | `a*a*b*b*c`\nL224: `poly_abc_plus_de`  | `a*b*c + d*e`\nL225: `poly_abcg_plus_deg`  | `a*b*c*g + d*e*g`\nL226: ## Repository layout\nL227: \nL228:     `src/zkduel/                    Python package: fields and modulus guard (fields.py), canonical\nL229:                                    workload expressions (workloads.py), CPU big-integer SumCheck\nL230:                                    reference (reference.py), 256-bit Triton backend (triton_backend/)\nL231:     benchmarks/field_sweep/        Prime-field drivers used for the paper:\nL232:       triton_sweep.py              Triton SumCheck for all 32 workloads and 4 widths\nL233:       compare_triton_cuda.py       Triton vs CUDA, joined CSV with checksum agreement\nL234:       resilient_compare_triton_cuda.py  Same, one subprocess per case, with a per-case timeout\nL235:       workload_specs.py            Workload metadata and aliases\nL236:       kernels/                     Triton expressions: poly.py (diagnostic tier), zk.py (zkPHIRE)\nL237:     benchmarks/u256/               Earlier 256-bit-only drivers (diagnostic-tier polynomials)\nL238:     benchmarks/wraparound_sweep/   Limb-width ablation with arithmetic mod 2^k (not a prime field)\nL239:     cuda/src/                      CUDA sources; field_sumcheck.cu is the paper's CUDA backend\nL240:     cuda/build/                    nvcc build scripts\nL241:     cuda/check/                    CPU checkers for the CUDA executables\nL242:     scripts/                       setup_env.sh, run_paper_experiments.sh, plot_zkduel.py\nL243:     tests/                         pytest suite (field guard, 256-bit arithmetic, CPU reference)\nL244:     docs/                          Benchmarks, implementation, reproduction, reading the results\nL245:     experiments/                   Data behind the paper and the scripts that produced it\nL246:       README.md                    Index: every dataset, the question it answers, its script\nL247:       h100/                        H100 PCIe, the primary platform: sweeps, diagnostics, validation,\nL248:                                    tools/, patches/, env/, logs/, FINDINGS.md, FINDINGS_TABLES.md\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - snarang181/zk-duel · GitHub (https://github.com/snarang181/zk-duel)\nciteturn5view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"pattern\":\"release\"}); Total lines: 417\nNo matching text found for \"release\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpeeding Up Sum-Check Proving (https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/1117)\nciteturn5view8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view0\",\"pattern\":\"2025\"}); Total lines: 82\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of 【0†tin foil†iacr.org】?\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Cryptology ePrint Archive】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Papers】 Updates from the last:\nL6:     * 【3†7 days】\nL7:     * 【4†31 days】\nL8:     * 【5†6 months】\nL9:     * 【6†365 days】\nL10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2025/1117\nL30: \nL31: ### 【17†Speeding Up Sum-Check Proving】\nL32: \nL33: Suyash Bagad, Ingonyama\nL34: \nL35: Quang Dao, Carnegie Mellon University\nL36: \nL37: Yuval Domb, Ingonyama\nL38: \nL39: Justin Thaler, Georgetown University, a16z crypto research\nL40: ##### Abstract\nL41: At the core of the fastest known SNARKs is the sum-check protocol. In this paper, we describe two complementary optimizations that significantly accelerate sum-check proving in key applications. The first targets scenarios where polynomial evaluations involve small values, such as unsigned 32-bit integers or elements of small subfields within larger extension fields.\nL42: This setting is common in applications such as Jolt, a state-of-the-art zero-knowledge virtual machine (zkVM) built on the sum-check protocol. Our core idea is to replace expensive multiplications over large fields with cheaper operations over smaller domains, yielding both asymptotic speedups and significant constant-factor improvements.\nL43: The second optimization addresses a common pattern where sum-check is applied to polynomials of the form $g(x) = \\mathsf{eq}(r, x) \\cdot p(x)$, where $\\mathsf{eq}$ is the multilinear extension of the equality function. We present a technique that substantially reduces the prover's cost associated with the equality polynomial component. We also describe how to combine both optimizations, which is essential for applications like Spartan within Jolt.\nL44: We have implemented and integrated our optimizations into the Jolt zkVM. Our benchmarks show consistent $2\\text{-}3\\times$ speedups for proving the first sum-check of Spartan within Jolt, with performance gains reaching 20$\\times$ or more when baseline methods approach their memory limits.\nL45: Note: This paper combines and extends the results from two prior pre-prints, 2024/1046 and 2024/1210.\nL46: ##### Metadata\nL47: \nL48: Available format(s)\nL49:     【17†PDF】\nL50: Category\nL51:     【18†Applications】\nL52: Publication info\nL53:     Preprint.\nL54: Keywords\nL55:     【19†snark】【20†sum-check】【21†zkvm】\nL56: Contact author(s)\nL57:     suyash @ ingonyama com\nL58: qvd @ andrew cmu edu\nL59: yuval @ ingonyama com\nL60: justin r thaler @ gmail com\nL61: History\nL62:     2025-06-17: revised\nL63:     2025-06-13: received\nL64:     【22†See all versions】\nL65: Short URL\nL66:     【23†https://ia.cr/2025/1117†ia.cr】\nL67: License\nL68: \nL69: 【24†CC BY†creativecommons.org】\nL70: BibTeX 【25†Image】Copy to clipboard\nL71: \nL72:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2025/1117,\nL73:           author = {Suyash Bagad and Quang Dao and Yuval Domb and Justin Thaler},\nL74:           title = {Speeding Up Sum-Check Proving},\nL75:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2025/1117},\nL76:           year = {2025},\nL77:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/1117}\nL78:     }\nL79: \nL80: \nL81: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n"
